1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,179
Here is a whole research experiment,
as Josh's example notebook writes it.

2
00:00:05,499 --> 00:00:08,592
Four calls: set up, train, predict, evaluate.

3
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:14,620
Behind them sits one file, speciesot_core.py,
about three hundred and fifty lines long.

4
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:18,388
Which lines matter,
and where do you even start reading?

5
00:00:18,567 --> 00:00:22,758
Here's the trick:
every class in that file does one of five jobs.

6
00:00:22,758 --> 00:00:27,271
Data, contract, conductor, model, and metric.
Let's learn to see them.

7
00:00:30,367 --> 00:00:33,683
Start with the word the whole file
is built from: class.

8
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:35,045
A class is a blueprint.

9
00:00:35,045 --> 00:00:37,115
It describes what a thing will know

10
00:00:37,115 --> 00:00:40,074
and what it can do,
but it isn't the thing itself.

11
00:00:40,234 --> 00:00:43,477
Calling a class like a function
builds an object from it.

12
00:00:43,797 --> 00:00:49,727
Line 115 does exactly that:
scGenModel, given its slice of the config.

13
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:53,527
The moment the object is built,
Python runs its init method.

14
00:00:53,847 --> 00:00:57,100
Each line of init writes one fact
onto the new object:

15
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:00,716
the learning rate, the latent size,
where to save the model.

16
00:01:00,867 --> 00:01:06,583
And self just means this particular object.
self.lr is this model's learning rate.

17
00:01:06,583 --> 00:01:09,720
Build two models, and each one keeps its own.

18
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:17,678
Near the top of the file
is a different kind of class: AbstractModel.

19
00:01:17,998 --> 00:01:22,115
Every method is marked abstract,
and its body only raises an error.

20
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:23,330
It's a contract.

21
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:27,718
Anything that calls itself a model
must know how to set up, train,

22
00:01:27,718 --> 00:01:32,251
and predict. The contract names the methods.
It doesn't fill them in.

23
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:37,136
Pause and predict: what happens
if you try to build an AbstractModel directly?

24
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,829
Python refuses.

25
00:01:41,879 --> 00:01:45,646
We ran it:
can't instantiate abstract class AbstractModel

26
00:01:45,646 --> 00:01:49,282
without an implementation for predict,
setup and train.

27
00:01:49,434 --> 00:01:52,805
IdentityModel and scGenModel fill in the blanks.

28
00:01:53,125 --> 00:01:56,619
Writing AbstractModel
in parentheses after the class name

29
00:01:56,619 --> 00:01:58,458
is how they sign the contract.

30
00:01:58,778 --> 00:02:00,350
That's called inheritance.

31
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:03,969
Look at train.
The contract's train takes training data,

32
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:07,920
but IdentityModel's takes nothing,
and Python still builds it.

33
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:10,342
The contract checks names, not inputs.

34
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,193
You'll also see abc.ABC on Experiment
and the data classes.

35
00:02:15,513 --> 00:02:18,351
With no abstract methods,
there's nothing to sign.

36
00:02:18,671 --> 00:02:22,494
Josh's note on line 19 says
the data classes can drop it,

37
00:02:22,494 --> 00:02:24,708
and the same goes for Experiment.

38
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:32,112
The Experiment class is the conductor.
It never does the science itself.

39
00:02:32,112 --> 00:02:36,624
It reads the config, builds the pieces,
and tells each one when to play.

40
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:41,795
Setup fills two lists:
models, from the models section of config.yaml,

41
00:02:41,795 --> 00:02:45,077
and evaluations, from the evaluations section.

42
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:47,686
Train and predict walk down the models list.

43
00:02:48,006 --> 00:02:52,657
Predict collects one answer from each model:
a set of predicted human cells.

44
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:57,889
Evaluate crosses the two lists.
Two models times two metrics: four numbers.

45
00:02:58,067 --> 00:02:59,422
One honest detail:

46
00:02:59,422 --> 00:03:04,239
as written, evaluate computes those numbers
and then drops them.

47
00:03:04,239 --> 00:03:08,627
Line 145 never stores them.
Remember that for episode five.

48
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:16,537
Here's the payoff. Line 137 calls model.predict,

49
00:03:16,537 --> 00:03:20,441
without ever asking which model it's talking to.

50
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,525
IdentityModel answers
by handing back the mouse cells unchanged.

51
00:03:24,845 --> 00:03:26,647
It's the do-nothing baseline:

52
00:03:26,647 --> 00:03:29,752
a real model
has to beat simply copying the mouse.

53
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:34,390
scGenModel will answer
by shifting the mouse cells toward human.

54
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:38,177
Same question, different answers.
That's polymorphism.

55
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:41,762
So think about it:
when you add CellOT as a third model,

56
00:03:41,762 --> 00:03:44,946
which lines of the Experiment
should have to change?

57
00:03:47,566 --> 00:03:48,945
Ideally, none.

58
00:03:48,995 --> 00:03:53,433
Today, setup still names each model by hand,
in a chain of if statements.

59
00:03:53,753 --> 00:03:55,758
Episode five pulls on that thread.

60
00:03:57,367 --> 00:04:01,570
Recap. A class is a blueprint,
and an object is built from it.

61
00:04:01,890 --> 00:04:05,047
Init writes the object's facts,
and self means this one.

62
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:07,262
An abstract class is a contract.

63
00:04:07,262 --> 00:04:10,651
It names the methods,
and Python won't build a model

64
00:04:10,651 --> 00:04:13,127
until every named method is filled in.

65
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:17,446
It checks names, not inputs.
Episode five shows what slips through.

66
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:18,929
The Experiment conducts,

67
00:04:18,929 --> 00:04:22,251
and models and metrics plug
into it through their contracts.

68
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:29,632
Your turn: pause here, open speciesot_core.py,
and label every class with one of the five jobs.

69
00:04:29,952 --> 00:04:31,766
This table is your answer key.
